.TH LIBESDM_RPC_CLIENT 3 "2026-10-16" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
libesdm_rpc_client \- client library for the ESDM server RPC interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <esdm/esdm_rpc_client.h>
.PP
.B pkg\-config \-\-cflags \-\-libs esdm_rpc_client
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B libesdm_rpc_client
gives an application access to a running
.BR esdm\-server (8)
over its RPC interface. This is the interface an application should use when
it wants random data from the system's ESDM: the entropy accounting stays with
the one daemon that owns the entropy sources, instead of every process running
an ESDM of its own as it would with
.BR libesdm (3).
.PP
The daemon offers two endpoints. The
.B unprivileged
one serves every user and carries the operations that only read state or
produce random data. The
.B privileged
one is restricted by the permissions of its socket and carries the operations
that change the state of the daemon. Each has its own set of init/fini calls,
and an application initializes only the endpoints it needs.
.SS Connection handling
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_init_unpriv_service(esdm_rpcc_interrupt_func_t " interrupt_func ");"
Set up access to the unprivileged endpoint. The call is reference counted, so
an application and a preloaded library may each call it without knowing about
one another; only the first call establishes the connection. The optional
.I interrupt_func
is consulted to decide whether an ongoing RPC call shall be interrupted, and is
registered only by the call that actually establishes the connection.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_rpcc_fini_unpriv_service(void);"
Drop one reference. The connection is torn down when the last one goes away.
.TP
.BI "void esdm_rpcc_force_fini_unpriv_service(void);"
Tear the connection down even though other users still hold references \- their
later calls then fail with
.BR \-EFAULT .
For process shutdown paths where the other users cannot be reached.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_init_priv_service(esdm_rpcc_interrupt_func_t " interrupt_func ");"
.TP
.BI "void esdm_rpcc_fini_priv_service(void);"
.TP
.BI "void esdm_rpcc_force_fini_priv_service(void);"
The same for the privileged endpoint.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_get_unpriv_service(esdm_rpc_client_connection_t **" rpc_conn ", void *" int_data ");"
.TP
.BI "void esdm_rpcc_put_unpriv_service(esdm_rpc_client_connection_t *" rpc_conn ");"
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_get_priv_service(esdm_rpc_client_connection_t **" rpc_conn ", void *" int_data ");"
.TP
.BI "void esdm_rpcc_put_priv_service(esdm_rpc_client_connection_t *" rpc_conn ");"
Check a connection handle of the respective endpoint out of the pool and
return it, for applications issuing requests on a handle themselves. The
service must be initialized; the get calls do not establish it and fail with
.B \-EFAULT
otherwise, or with
.B \-ESHUTDOWN
while it is being torn down. The handle belongs to the pool and must not be
freed. Passing NULL to a put call is a no\-op.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_set_max_online_nodes(uint32_t " nodes ");"
Limit the number of connection pool nodes the client keeps. Call before
initializing a service.
.PP
Every RPC call listed below exists in two forms: a plain one, and one suffixed
.BR _int ()
that takes an additional
.I void *int_data
argument. That pointer is passed to the registered interrupt function and lets
the application tell its own concurrent requests apart. The plain form is the
.BR _int ()
form with a NULL
.IR int_data .
.SS Encryption
A library built with DTLS (meson option
.BR dtls )
asks the server to encrypt every new connection with DTLS 1.2, with a STARTDTLS
request in the clear, and does the handshake when the server agrees. All
requests and answers then pass encrypted. The DTLS is opportunistic: a server
that does not agree \- one started with
.BR \-\-no_dtls ,
one built without DTLS, or one of ESDM 1.2.3 or before, which does not know the
request \- is used in the clear, without the call failing: on the same
connection once the server answered, on a new one should it close the
connection instead. A server answering neither the request nor the one the
library sends right behind it is waited for a second at the most \- the time
asking for DTLS and the handshake may take together \-, and the call goes on in
the clear on a new connection then, as it does should the handshake fail. A
connection whose server did not agree or did not complete the handshake does
not ask again for a minute. The DTLS is unauthenticated, as described in
.BR esdm\-server (8).
With debug logging, every connection is logged with whether it is encrypted.
.PP
A new connection costs the handshake, a fraction of a millisecond, and the first
one of a process a few milliseconds more to set up the TLS stack. As the server
closes idle connections and the library reconnects after half of that time,
this cost comes once per call after a second of idleness at the most.
.PP
The TLS stack takes random numbers from the operating system during the
handshake. In a process whose
.BR getrandom (2)
or
.BR getentropy (3)
is served by this library \- one preloading
.BR libesdm_getrandom ,
for instance \- that is a call of this library made while it is in the middle of
setting up the connection. Such a call fails at once with
.BR \-EDEADLK ,
which libesdm_getrandom answers from the kernel, rather than waiting for the
connection it is called for.
.PP
A child created with
.BR fork (2)
does not use the connections of its parent, encrypted or not: it closes them in
its process, leaves the connection of the parent alone, and connects anew.
.SS Obtaining random data
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_rpcc_get_random_bytes(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Random data without a guarantee that the ESDM is fully seeded. A request the
ESDM answers with
.B \-EAGAIN
\- the DRNG serving it reached its maximum output without full reseed \- is
sent again, at once and, should that be answered the same way, after a second.
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_rpcc_get_random_bytes_full(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Random data, blocking until the ESDM is fully seeded.
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_rpcc_get_random_bytes_full_timeout(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", struct timespec *" ts ");"
The same, bounded by the relative timeout
.IR ts ,
after which it returns
.BR \-ETIMEDOUT ,
or the error of the last connection attempt when the server could not be
reached until then.
A
.I ts
with a negative field or a
.I tv_nsec
of a second or more is refused with
.BR \-EINVAL .
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_rpcc_get_random_bytes_pr(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Random data with prediction resistance.
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_rpcc_get_random_bytes_pr_nonblock(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
The same, but where the ESDM cannot serve prediction resistant data right away
\- it is not operational yet, another prediction resistant request is in
flight, or the server thread already spent its share of the current round on
collections from the entropy sources, see
.BR esdm_rpcc_get_seed ()
\- it returns the data obtained so far, or
.B \-EAGAIN
if that is none, rather than waiting.
.TP
.BI "ssize_t esdm_rpcc_get_seed(uint8_t *" buf ", size_t " buflen ", unsigned int " flags ");"
Seed material taken directly from the entropy sources, for callers seeding a
DRNG of their own.
.I flags
takes
.B ESDM_GET_SEED_NONBLOCK
and
.BR ESDM_GET_SEED_FULLY_SEEDED ;
see
.BR libesdm (3)
for the layout of the returned buffer. The server collects the seed without
waiting, and answers
.B \-EAGAIN
when it cannot right away: the ESDM is not fully seeded yet, another seed is
being collected, or the server thread already spent its share of the current
round on collections from the entropy sources \- requests for seeds and for
prediction resistant data share it. With
.BR ESDM_GET_SEED_NONBLOCK ,
that is returned; without it, the request is sent again after a pause until
it is served.
.SS Status
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_status(char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Status of the ESDM and its entropy sources as human readable text. A report
that does not fit into
.I buflen
is cut short there, still NUL\-terminated, and
.B \-EMSGSIZE
is returned. The server caps the report at just under 64 KiB
.RB ( ESDM_RPC_MAX_STATUS_DATA );
a longer one arrives cut short in the same way, with the same return value.
On any other error,
.I buf
holds the empty string rather than the report of an earlier call.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_status_json(char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
The same information as a JSON document. A document is never cut short: when it
does not fit into
.I buflen
or exceeds the cap of the server,
.B \-EMSGSIZE
is returned. On any error,
.I buf
holds the empty string rather than a partial or stale document.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_drng_status_json(uint32_t " node ", char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Status of the DRNG instance of
.I node
as a JSON document, with the error handling of
.BR esdm_rpcc_status_json ().
.B \-ENODEV
means the node has no DRNG instance.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_drng_status_pr_json(char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
The same for the DRNG instance serving prediction resistant requests.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_jent_status(char *" buf ", size_t " buflen ");"
Internal status of the Jitter RNG entropy source as a JSON document, with the
error handling of
.BR esdm_rpcc_status_json ().
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_is_fully_seeded(bool *" fully_seeded ");"
Whether the ESDM is fully seeded.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_get_ent_lvl(unsigned int *" entlvl ");"
Entropy level of the ESDM, in bits.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_get_poolsize(unsigned int *" poolsize ");"
Size of the entropy pool, in bits.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_get_write_wakeup_thresh(unsigned int *" thresh ");"
Entropy level below which polling writers are woken.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_get_min_reseed_secs(unsigned int *" seconds ");"
Maximum time in seconds between two reseeds of a DRNG.
.SS Contributing data
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_write_data(const uint8_t *" data_buf ", size_t " data_buf_len ");"
Write data into the auxiliary pool without crediting entropy. This uses the
unprivileged endpoint and is open to every user.
.SS Administration
These require the privileged endpoint.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_set_write_wakeup_thresh(unsigned int " thresh ");"
Set the write wakeup threshold.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_set_min_reseed_secs(unsigned int " seconds ");"
Set that time. Zero seconds reseeds before every request, values above one hour
are capped to it.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_set_pkcs11_config(const char *" token_label ", const char *" pin ");"
Set the token label and user PIN of the PKCS#11 entropy source.
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_selftest(struct esdm_rpcc_selftest_result *" result ");"
Run the self tests of the server now. Returns 0 if every self test passed and
the ESDM hands out random bits, otherwise the error of the failing test, or
.B \-EOPNOTSUPP
where an earlier failure still holds the output back. If
.I result
is not NULL, it receives the state of the crypto and the entropy source self
tests and the number of entropy sources tested and failed, on error too.
.SS Linux random device IOCTLs
These mirror the
.BR ioctl (2)
commands of the Linux random device and back the implementation in
.BR esdm\-cuse\-random (8).
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_rnd_get_ent_cnt(unsigned int *" entcnt ");"
.B RNDGETENTCNT
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_rnd_add_to_ent_cnt(unsigned int " entcnt ");"
.B RNDADDTOENTCNT
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_rnd_add_entropy(const uint8_t *" entropy_buf ", size_t " entropy_buf_len ", uint32_t " entropy_cnt ");"
.B RNDADDENTROPY
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_rnd_clear_pool(void);"
.BR RNDCLEARPOOL / RNDZAPENTCNT
.TP
.BI "int esdm_rpcc_rnd_reseed_crng(void);"
.B RNDRESEEDCRNG
.SH RETURN VALUE
Functions returning
.B int
return 0 on success, functions returning
.B ssize_t
return the number of bytes produced. Both return a negative errno value on
error.
.B \-EINTR
means the call was interrupted through the interrupt function and may simply be
repeated; the
.B esdm_invoke()
macro declared in the header does that up to five times. A call of the
privileged endpoint interrupted after its request was sent returns
.B \-EINPROGRESS
instead, as the server may still apply the request; neither
.B esdm_invoke()
nor the caller should repeat it.
.B \-EFAULT
means the service was never initialized or has been torn down underneath the
caller.
.B \-EDEADLK
means the call was made from inside the DTLS of a connection of the library,
see
.BR Encryption .
.B \-EMSGSIZE
means the request does not fit into one message of just under 64 KiB, which
limits the entropy buffer of
.BR esdm_rpcc_rnd_add_entropy ().
.BR esdm_rpcc_write_data ()
splits its data into messages itself.
.PP
.B \-ETIMEDOUT
means the server did not answer in time. A request of the unprivileged endpoint
whose answer is late is sent again on a fresh connection, which may be served by
a less busy server thread, until about 6.4 seconds have passed in all; one the
server closed the connection on without answering is sent again once.
A request of the privileged endpoint is waited for up to 60 seconds and never
sent a second time \- neither after a timeout nor after a connection reset
.RB ( \-ECONNRESET ),
since the server may already have applied it, and applying it twice \- such as
crediting entropy twice \- is worse than reporting the failure.
.SH THREAD SAFETY
The library maintains a pool of connections. A call takes one out of the pool
for its duration, so calls from several threads proceed concurrently as long as
the pool has free entries and block on each other otherwise. Applications that
manage connection handles themselves with
.BR esdm_rpcc_get_unpriv_service ()
or
.BR esdm_rpcc_get_priv_service ()
must return them with the matching put call
as soon as the request completed, since a checked\-out handle is held
exclusively.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <esdm/esdm_rpc_client.h>
#include <errno.h>

int get_random(uint8_t *buf, size_t buflen)
{
	ssize_t ret;

	if (esdm_rpcc_init_unpriv_service(NULL))
		return \-EFAULT;

	esdm_invoke(esdm_rpcc_get_random_bytes_full(buf, buflen));

	esdm_rpcc_fini_unpriv_service();

	return (ret == (ssize_t)buflen) ? 0 : (int)ret;
}
.fi
.PP
Note that
.B esdm_invoke()
assigns to a variable named
.I ret
in the enclosing scope.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libesdm (3),
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3),
.BR libesdm_aux_client (3),
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-tool (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Stephan Mueller <smueller@chronox.de>
